<ContentLayout structuredData={__sd} title="GaN charger not charging laptop full speed: why wattage drops | StackGeist" description="A GaN charger delivering less power than expected is commonly limited by port allocation, cable capability, the laptop's supported USB-C PD profiles, a dock in the path, or thermal protection. Diagnose the power path before replacing the brick." active="guides">
  <article class="article-page">
    <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides">Guides</a> / GaN charger not charging full speed</p>
    <p class="page-kicker">Power troubleshooting</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">GaN charger not charging laptop full speed: why wattage drops.</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">A GaN charger delivering less power than the box advertises is almost never broken. It is negotiating a lower USB-PD profile because a second device is sharing the total wattage, the cable cannot carry the current, or the laptop itself is refusing to draw more. Diagnose which side is limiting the wattage before buying a new charger.</p>
    <div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Unplug everything except the laptop and use the charger's documented highest-output USB-C port. Test with a known-rated cable that covers the laptop's required power. If nothing changes, compare the laptop's documented USB-C charging limit, bypass any dock, and check for thermal or firmware constraints.</p></div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Check the per-port wattage table, not the total rating.</h2><p>Multi-port GaN chargers can redistribute output as ports become active. The exact combinations are model-specific, so use the charger's printed output table or official manual rather than assuming a standard split.</p><ul class="article-list"><li>Find the label or sticker on the charger itself and read the per-port table.</li><li>Use the highest-wattage port for the laptop and leave the others empty.</li><li>If the table shows 65W max on any single port, the charger cannot deliver 100W to one device regardless of what the box says.</li></ul></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Confirm the cable supports the wattage you expect.</h2><p>For USB-C to USB-C charging, a 3A cable supports up to 60W at 20V. Charging above 60W requires 5A cable capability with electronic marking. Current USB-IF certified-cable branding uses 60W or 240W power markings, while older 100W-rated products remain common.[1]</p><p>Do not infer power capability from cable thickness. If the rating is unknown, substitute a known-rated cable. Use the cable that shipped with the charger, or buy a rated 100W or 240W USB-C to USB-C cable of the length you actually need.</p><div class="note">Power rating and data rate are separate cable properties. Verify the manufacturer's documented rating rather than judging the cable by thickness, price, or connector appearance.</div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Test with one port active and nothing else plugged in.</h2><p>Unplug every other device from the charger and use only the highest-wattage port listed on the label. If charging speed jumps to what you expect, the charger was sharing its total output across multiple ports and the fix is either a solo charger for the laptop or accepting the split. If nothing changes, the cable, laptop, or a protocol mismatch is the limit.</p></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Verify the laptop does not require proprietary charging.</h2><p>Some laptops accept USB-C Power Delivery but at a lower maximum than their proprietary adapter, while others require a specific USB-C PD profile. Search the exact model's power and USB-C specifications and compare them with the charger's supported output profiles.[2]</p><div class="article-cards"><div><strong>USB-C PD supported</strong><p>Match the charger's supported voltage/current profiles and wattage to the laptop's documented USB-C input.</p></div><div><strong>Lower USB-C ceiling</strong><p>Some systems accept USB-C charging but reach full performance only with a higher-power proprietary adapter.</p></div><div><strong>Model-specific behavior</strong><p>Use the manufacturer's documentation for the exact laptop; vendor and product-family generalizations are not reliable.</p></div></div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 05</p><h2>Clean the USB-C port on both ends and inspect for damage.</h2><p>USB-C ports collect lint, dust, and pocket debris that silently blocks full pin contact. A dirty port can force the charger to negotiate down to a lower current or voltage to protect the connection. Do not insert metal objects or improvised tools into the connector. With the device powered down, inspect the port and use compressed air if the manufacturer recommends it.</p><ul class="article-list"><li>Power off the laptop before cleaning the port.</li><li>Use compressed air at an angle to blow debris out, not deeper in.</li><li>Inspect for bent pins or discoloration inside the port — signs of physical damage or water entry.</li></ul></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 06</p><h2>Let the charger cool and move it off the laptop.</h2><p>Chargers include thermal protection and may reduce or stop output outside their allowed temperature range. Test on a hard, unobstructed surface at normal room temperature and let an overheated charger cool before retesting. Move the charger to a hard surface with airflow and wait five minutes before testing again.</p></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>When the diagnosis says the charger is fine.</h2><p>Most wattage drops resolve at Step 01, 02, or 03. If the charger's per-port limit or cable rating was the ceiling, accepting the split or buying a rated cable is cheaper than a new charger. If the laptop's documented USB-C charging ceiling is below its full-power requirement, use the manufacturer's supported higher-power charging method. If measurements with compatible equipment show the charger cannot meet its documented single-port output with a supported load and cable, contact the manufacturer or retailer for support or warranty service.</p><div class="cards">
